          "say": "Halve the corner angle, not the 90 in the middle.",
          "write": "middle is always 90°"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Kites and trapeziums",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "A kite has 70 and 50, and two equal angles. All four make 360.",
          "write": "360 − 70 − 50 = 240"
        },
        {
          "say": "Share 240 between the equal pair. Each is 120.",
          "write": "240 ÷ 2 = 120°"
        },
        {
          "say": "In a trapezium, angles along a slanted side add to 180.",
          "write": "co-interior: 180°"
        },
        {
          "say": "An isosceles trapezium has equal base angles and equal diagonals.",
          "write": "isosceles trapezium"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Is it a parallelogram?",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Opposite sides parallel? Or opposite sides equal? Parallelogram.",
          "write": "opp. sides ∥ or ="
        },
        {
          "say": "Opposite angles equal? Or diagonals bisect each other? Parallelogram.",
          "write": "opp. angles = or bisect"
        },
        {
          "say": "One pair of sides both equal and parallel also works.",
          "write": "1 pair = and ∥"
        },
        {
          "say": "Equal diagonals alone prove nothing. An isosceles trapezium has them.",
          "write": "✗ equal diagonals alone"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "An algebra example",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Opposite angles: 3x plus 10, and 5x minus 30. They're equal.",
          "write": "3x + 10 = 5x − 30"
        },
        {
          "say": "Solve it. x equals 20, so angle A is 70 degrees.",
          "write": "x = 20, ∠A = 70°"
        },
        {
          "say": "Angle B is next to A. They add to 180, so B is 110.",
          "write": "∠B = 180 − 70 = 110°"
        }
      ]
    },
